course: history
lesson: 17
title: Native peoples of North America
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People first came to the Americas at least 15,000 years ago, probably from Asia during the Ice Age, across a land bridge or along the coast by boat. When Europeans arrived much later, tens of millions of people already lived there.
explain: There was no single Native American way of life, just as there is no single European one. Think of how different Norway, Spain and Greece are: the Inuit of the Arctic, the Lakota of the Plains and the Pueblo peoples of the desert were just as different.
explain: Some hunted bison, some farmed corn, beans and squash, some built towns of stone in cliffs, and at Cahokia a city with huge earth mounds. The Haudenosaunee nations joined in a league of peace to settle quarrels by talking.
explain: Europeans brought diseases like smallpox that killed very many people, and later settlers pushed Native nations off their lands with wars and broken treaties. But this is not only the past: millions of Native people live today, keeping their languages and nations alive.
fact: People first came to the Americas at least 15,000 years ago, probably from Asia across a land bridge or along the coast by boat during the Ice Age.
fact: The Americas were not empty when Europeans arrived; tens of millions of people lived there.
fact: Native peoples of North America were many nations with hundreds of languages, such as the Cherokee, Navajo, Lakota, Haudenosaunee, Inuit and Cree.
fact: Feathered headdresses and tipis belonged to some Plains peoples, not to all Native Americans.
fact: On the Great Plains nations such as the Lakota followed huge herds of bison, using almost every part for food, clothing, tools and tipi covers, and after the Spanish brought horses they became expert riders.
fact: Many eastern nations farmed corn, beans and squash together, called the Three Sisters, because the plants help each other grow; corn, first grown in the Americas, now feeds people all over the world.
fact: Five, later six, nations, including the Mohawk and Seneca, formed the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, a league of peace with a council to settle quarrels by talking.
fact: In the dry Southwest the Ancestral Puebloans built houses of stone and adobe in cliffs and canyons, like Mesa Verde, farmed by carefully collecting the little rain that fell, and Pueblo peoples still live there today.
fact: Near today's St Louis stood Cahokia, a city of perhaps 15,000 people about 900 years ago, with huge earth mounds.
fact: The Inuit of the Arctic hunted seals and whales, used kayaks and dog sleds, and in winter some built snow houses called igloos, but most did not live in igloos all year; they also lived in houses of stone, turf and skins.
fact: Europeans brought diseases like smallpox that Native peoples had never met, and very many died, often before they ever saw a European, as the illness spread ahead from village to village.
fact: As settlers spread west, many Native nations were pushed off their lands by wars and broken treaties; on the Trail of Tears in the 1830s the Cherokee were forced to walk far west, and thousands died.
fact: Millions of Native people live in the USA and Canada today, keeping their languages, ceremonies and nations alive, and many nations have their own governments.
exercise: At least how long ago did people first come to the Americas? | At least 15,000 years ago | About 500 years ago | About 1,000 years ago | About 3 million years ago
exercise: From which continent did the first Americans probably come? | Asia | Europe | Africa directly by boat | Australia
exercise: Were the Americas empty when Europeans arrived? | No, tens of millions of people lived there | Yes, nobody at all lived there before the ships came | Only a few hundred people lived there | Only in the far north
exercise: Which of these is a Native nation of North America? | The Cherokee | The Maori | The Zulu | The Sami
exercise: Which animal was at the centre of Lakota life on the Plains? | The bison | The wild camel | The llama of Peru | The elephant
exercise: Who brought horses to the Americas? | The Spanish | The Vikings from Norway | The Inuit of the far north | The Chinese on ships
exercise: What were the Three Sisters? | Corn, beans and squash grown together | Three famous Cherokee queens who ruled together | Three rivers of the Plains | Three gods of the Inuit
exercise: Why were the Three Sisters planted together? | The plants help each other grow | It looked pretty in pictures and paintings of the fields | It was the law | A king ordered it
exercise: What was the Haudenosaunee Confederacy? | A league of nations settling quarrels by talking | A big trading ship that sailed the Great Lakes every summer | A Spanish fort | A single Inuit village
exercise: Name one Haudenosaunee nation. | The Mohawk | The Lakota of the Plains | The Inuit of the Arctic | The Aztecs of Mexico
exercise: Where did the Ancestral Puebloans build their homes? | In cliffs and canyons of the dry Southwest | In the Arctic ice, in houses of frozen snow blocks | On floating islands | In tall trees in the jungle
exercise: What was Cahokia? | A city with huge earth mounds | A kind of long birch-bark canoe for rivers | A Spanish explorer | A snow house
exercise: About how many people lived in Cahokia? | Perhaps 15,000 | About 15 | About 15 million | About 150
exercise: What did the Inuit hunt? | Seals and whales | Bison and deer | Llamas and alpacas | Camels
exercise: What killed very many Native people after Europeans came? | Diseases like smallpox | Volcanoes | A long ice age | Wild bison
exercise: What was the Trail of Tears? | The forced march of the Cherokee far west | A river in Canada | A famous trading route for furs that ran from Canada down to Mexico | A Lakota hunting trail
exercise: Are Native nations only part of the past? | No, millions of Native people live today | Yes, they all disappeared long ago, in the 1800s | Yes, only in museums | No, but they all live in Europe now
test: in cowboy films all native americans wear feather headdresses and live in tipis. right? | No, that was only some Plains peoples | Yes, every nation did, from Florida to Alaska | Yes, even the Inuit | No, none of them ever did
test: My mate says the Inuit lived in igloos all year round. What is true? | Many lived in houses of stone, turf and skins; igloos were for winter | Yes, all year in igloos | They lived in tipis | They lived in pyramids
test: Why did many Native people die of smallpox before ever seeing a European? | The disease spread ahead from village to village | They were so scared of ships that they fell ill and died | Smallpox came from the bison | They caught it from the Inuit
test: If corn, beans and squash had never been shared with the world, what might be missing today? | Corn and other foods that now feed the world | All the wheat that grows in Europe and Russia today | Rice in China | Nothing at all
test: Cowboy films show settlers as heroes. How was it for Native nations? | They lost their homes and lands | They were glad to move to new lands in the west | They had no homes to lose | They won every war
test: A Lakota hunter after 1600 rides a horse. Where did the horse come from? | The Spanish brought horses to the Americas | Horses had always lived wild on the Plains since the ice age | The Vikings brought them | The Inuit bred them
test: Why might the Haudenosaunee council be called wise? | It settled quarrels by talking, not fighting | It had the most weapons and the biggest army | It never met | It obeyed a king from Spain who sent them orders
